 "observations": "GATE PASSED EXACTLY. The TOP_T=32 / MAX_QUERIES_PER_HEAD=64 arm reproduced DIAG-KEYCURVE / MECH-BUDGET A_t32 to all 16 printed digits on all four required values (k=12 QA 1.9560121297836304 / MT 2.2720184326171875; k=16 QA 2.034916400909424 / MT 2.330503463745117), the four evaluated checkpoints are sha256-identical to MECH-BUDGET's (c167a55174409c1a, ba592c96c44b4828, 96eb96a3d1b0f4e3, 3065c076062bf2ea), and the standalone routing-mass probe reproduced MECH-BUDGET's t32 numbers to all 17 digits (eval mass_on_S MT 0.24302008119945334 / QA 0.23122564051411448, ratio 1.0510083598822118), so the harness, the import pin and the base config are sound and every arm below is comparable to both sessions. DETERMINACY WAS ACHIEVED AND VERIFIED PER ARM: n_queries_used_min = n_queries_used_max = the cap in every (layer, head) of every arm, out of 57344 available - 64 for the gate (2.0:1), 512 for TOP_T=128 (4.0:1), 512 for TOP_T=64 (8.0:1), 1024 for TOP_T=256 (4.0:1) - so the data block alone determines the solve in all four new arms. WHAT \"UNDER-DETERMINED\" ACTUALLY MEANT, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED (determinacy_check.log, the real solver from the frozen snapshot on same-shape designs, no GPU): with DELTA_WEIGHT>0 the stacked design [X_data ; sqrt(w) I_t] is (n+t) x t, so the solver never enters the min-norm n<k branch the board attributed the effect to - it always takes the normal equations. The underdetermination lives in the DATA block: at t=128, n=64 that block has rank 64, leaving a 64-dimensional null space per head in which the solution is pinned to its prior value to |dV|/|V| = lambda_eff/(w+lambda_eff) = 0.0004, i.e. HALF of every head's 128 support directions were written carrying no new information, which is also why MECH-BUDGET's t128 had the lowest am/mean_mse of its three arms. At n=512/t=128 and n=1024/t=256 the data block is full column rank. RAISING THE QUERY CAP DOES NOT RESCUE t128: same top_t, n 64 -> 512, MT moves -0.0042 (k=8), +0.1053 (k=10), +0.0504 (k=12), -0.0347 (k=16) and the own-optimum MT is +0.0504 (1.02x the +-0.049 paired resolution, on the WORSE side); QA own-optimum +0.0405. THE DECISIVE, FULLY CONTROLLED CONTRAST - support doubled with everything else held, both solves determined, matched n=512 and matched DELTA_WEIGHT=1e-2 (A_t128q512 vs C_t64q512): MT deltas are +0.0346, +0.0284, -0.0230, -0.0185 at k=8/10/12/16, i.e. all four INSIDE the measured resolution (0.37x-0.70x) and not even consistent in sign, own-optimum +0.0307 (0.62x, does not clear); QA is +0.1755, +0.1729, +0.1335, +0.1321, all clearing at 2.45x-3.26x. Pushing support further at the same 4:1 ratio (B_t256q1024 vs A_t128q512) repeats it exactly: MT -0.0254, -0.0203, +0.0165, +0.0160 (0.32x-0.51x, none clears) while QA costs +0.0892 to +0.1429 (1.66x-2.65x, all clear). THE CONTROL THE BRIEF ASKED FOR SHOWS THE QUERY COUNT IS NOT A FREE CHANGE: at TOP_T=64, which was already exactly determined at n=64, raising n alone to 512 makes BOTH axes worse by more than the resolution at every k - MT +0.1450, +0.1724, +0.1424, +0.1300 (2.63x-3.49x, and the own-optimum +0.1450 also clears the conservative composite +-0.1055) and QA +0.1152 to +0.1671 (2.14x-3.10x). So the cross-session t128 comparison is confounded by a query-count effect of about -0.14 MT in the wrong direction, and only the within-session, matched-n A-vs-C contrast isolates support - which is what item 3 of the brief was for, and it returns a null on MT. DELTA_WEIGHT was held at the base config's 1e-2 in all four primary arms (stated choice: it keeps A-vs-C single-variable, and MECH-QUERIES-B had already measured the w = 1e-2*n/64 line at top_t=32 and found it did not improve MT); the explicit both-ways check D_t128q512dw repeats A at w=8e-2 = 1e-2*512/64 and reproduces MECH-QUERIES-B's signature at t=128 - |v|max falls 130.0 -> 82.0, am/mean_mse rises 0.002118 -> 0.005363 (gentler write, worse fit) - and recovers part of the query-count damage (MT -0.0519 at k=8, -0.0940 at k=10, +0.0761 at k=12, -0.0019 at k=16; own-optimum -0.0480, 0.97x, just inside the resolution), but its best MT is still 2.3595, which is +0.0875 (1.77x) worse than the incumbent's 2.2720 and +0.128 worse than MECH-BUDGET's t64 best 2.2318, so the support null is not an artefact of the diluted trust region. NO NEW ARM IS A PASS AND NONE BEATS THE INCUMBENT: best MT is 2.4075 (t128/q512, k=12), 2.3768 (t64/q512, k=8), 2.3860 (t256/q1024, k=8), 2.3595 (t128/q512 w-scaled, k=8), i.e. +0.0875 to +0.1355 above the incumbent's 2.2720 (1.77x-2.74x resolution) and +0.128 to +0.176 above MECH-BUDGET's t64 best 2.2318; MT shortfalls against the 2.02 target are 0.340-0.388. QA stays inside the 2.52 budget at every arm's own MT optimum, but B_t256q1024 breaks it at k>=10 (2.5172 / 2.5488 / 2.5827) and every new arm costs QA against the incumbent (own-optimum +0.257 to +0.524, 4.8x-9.7x). The internal signature is the same one every mechanism in this project produces: bandwidth up and fit better while CE gets worse - eval-time mass_on_S (MT) 0.2430 (t32) -> 0.3065 (t64/q512) -> 0.3098 (t128/q512) -> 0.3940 (t256/q1024), solve-time ref_mass_on_S 0.3136 -> 0.3984 -> 0.4247 -> 0.4944, am/mean_mse 0.003941 -> 0.003504 -> 0.002118 -> 0.001424, while the MT/QA selectivity ratio stays in the familiar band at 1.0510 / 1.0621 / 1.0616 / 1.0580 / 1.0609 and total cartridge mass (MT) is near-flat at 0.6711 / 0.6751 / 0.6790 / 0.6872 / 0.6984. |v|max is 74.0 / 130.0 / 163.0 / 150.0 / 82.0. COST: nothing OOMed and no arm came near the 30-minute limit; solve_s is 309.0 / 349.2 / 296.2 / 403.6 / 388.5 s (0.96x-1.31x the gate arm) across an 8x budget and 16x query-count increase, and gradient_steps = 0 in every arm by construction. The raw phase2_e2e_s (574 / 605 / 464 / 438 / 480 s) is again contaminated by cold-CUDA JIT - the gate ran first and cold and the t256 arm had the LOWEST e2e despite the largest budget - and this session's gate solve_s (309.0 s) differs by 19% from MECH-BUDGET's byte-identical t32 run (367.4 s), so solve_s is only comparable within a session. CAVEATS NOT ARGUED AWAY: (i) raising MAX_QUERIES_PER_HEAD changes three coupled things at once - the data block's rank, the trust region's relative pull (which decays like 1/n) and the effective ridge, since RIDGE_SCALE=spectral scales lambda with the design's spectral norm (measured on same-shape designs: lambda_eff 1.8e-06 at t32/q64, 2.6e-05 at t128/q512, 1.0e-04 at t256/q1024); only the matched-n A-vs-C and B-vs-A contrasts hold all three fixed, and those are the two that return the MT null. (ii) Every arm is one seed (AM_SEED_OFFSET unset, so all five share the same per-document reference draw); the DIAG-PERDOC reference-draw term is not sampled here, and under DIAG-NOISE's conservative composite (+-0.106 MT / +-0.197 QA) the only MT contrast that still clears is the queries-alone one. (iii) The routing-mass probe was run on the k=16 snapshot only, while three of the four new arms take their MT minimum at k=8. (iv) The k grid is {8,10,12,16}, so each arm's argmin is located on four points, and D_t128q512dw's curve is non-monotone in k (MT 2.3595 / 2.3697 / 2.4836 / 2.4685). PROVENANCE: env-only, no source file edited; imports pinned to the frozen snapshot /tmp/amsnap_budgetb (git archive HEAD cartridges examples), manifest b71eaf15e00306be667183893a745c19460b9261739d179433ddc919c8abe1c9 identical before and after and identical to MECH-BUDGET's snapshot; the import probe run from /tmp printed /tmp/amsnap_budgetb/cartridges; the repo HEAD moved from 522fcf3 to 5938fee during the run while MECH-INFOGATE held five modified files, and the pin held; one GPU (device 1) claimed via flock; 5 solves + 40 standalone evals + 1 eval-time routing-mass probe, all from the snapshot."
